# Trie and state roots

**`mpt` is not a node tree.** `foundation/trie/types.lisp` defines it as a flat
key→value hash table, and `mpt-put` / `mpt-delete` only touch that table. Every
root call rebuilds the entire node tree from all entries and then recursively
RLP-encodes the whole thing.

The consequence is that a state root costs O(all accounts) no matter how few
changed, and no amount of caching at the state-db layer fixes it. Making leaf
updates incremental bought roughly 27%; the residual is entirely the rebuild
and re-encode.

So **persistent trie nodes are an XL rewrite of the trie module**, not an
M-sized optimisation of the state layer. It means turning `mpt` into a real
node tree whose put and delete mutate nodes along a single path, and whose
nodes cache their own encoding and hash, invalidated only along the dirty path.
The trie backs state, storage, transactions, receipts and withdrawals, so the
blast radius is every root in the system. It also unlocks range proofs, and
therefore snap sync.

Guard rails if you attempt it: `*verify-incremental-root*` compares every flush
against a full rebuild and is bound true by the suite, and the EEST fixture
runs sit in the cold gates.

**Settled — do not re-litigate without new measurements.** `state-db-copy` is
not a bottleneck, at 0.05–0.75ms, despite deep-cloning the world on every
CALL/CREATE through snapshots. It has already been suspected once and refuted
by measurement.
